WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.775
Let us finish Module 3 by reviewing the main ideas
SELECT builds

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.750
a result from stored data An explicit column list
helps define exactly

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:15.238
what the result should contain WHERE chooses which
rows remain You practiced

00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:19.938
comparisons BETWEEN for ranges IN for lists of
values ILIKE for case-insensitive

00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:24.775
text patterns and IS NULL for missing values ORDER
BY controls the

00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:29.412
result order LIMIT keeps a chosen number of rows and
DISTINCT can

00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:35.162
remove repeated result values INSERT adds rows
UPDATE changes

00:00:35.188 --> 00:00:40.375
existing rows DELETE can remove rows while a
soft-delete pattern can preserve

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:44.675
a row and mark it inactive Because these statements
change data you

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.225
practiced previewing target rows using stable keys
and verifying the result with

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.862
RETURNING Transactions give you control over whether
a group of changes is

00:00:53.888 --> 00:00:58.838
kept or undone BEGIN starts a transaction COMMIT
keeps it and ROLLBACK

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:04.225
discards it Database constraints provide another
layer of protection by enforcing rules

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.762
and relationships You also worked with JSONB and
with INSERT INTO SELECT

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.700
which connects SQL to broader data preparation and
pipeline workflows If you

00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:17.600
remember one overall habit make it this ask a clear
question write

00:01:17.612 --> 00:01:21.738
the smallest useful statement run it inspect the
result and verify before

00:01:21.762 --> 00:01:25.075
you keep a change In the next part of the course you

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.412
can build on these single-table skills with
aggregation grouping joins and more

00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:30.488
analytical SQL
